.TH SHSELECT 1 "2026-11-17" "shselect 1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
shselect \- terminal selector and pager

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shselect
.RB [ \-bchmnsv ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR height ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR text ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shselect
is a pager and selector.
It reads input from a pipe or standard input and displays the content in
an interactive terminal interface.
The user can move through the list, select entries, and return the
selected line(s) or line number(s) to the calling script.

.PP
The tool is intended for use in POSIX shell scripts where an interactive
selector is useful.
Depending on the options,
.B shselect
can return selected lines, their line numbers, or both.
When multiselect mode is enabled, multiple entries may be marked and
returned.

.PP
.B shselect
is part of the
.B shui-tools
collection.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Show usage information.

.TP
.BI \-r " lines"
Set the height of the selection area in lines.
A value of
.B 0
uses the entire terminal height.
The default is
.BR 4 .

.TP
.BI \-p " text"
Set the status bar text.

.TP
.B \-c
Show a cursor in the first column instead of highlighting the current
line using reverse video.

.TP
.B \-b
Display the status bar in reverse video.

.TP
.B \-m
Enable multiselect mode.
When enabled, entries may be marked or unmarked.

.TP
.B \-n
Return only the line number of the selected entry.

.TP
.B \-s
Return only the selected line itself.

.PP
By default, both the line number and the line content are returned.

.TP
.B \-v
Leaving with
.B x
or
.B q
also returns the string on which the cursor was positioned.

.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
While viewing a list, the following keys are available:

.TP
.B Right, PageDown
Move forward one window.

.TP
.B Left, PageUp
Move backward one window.

.TP
.B Down
Move down one line.

.TP
.B Up
Move up one line.

.TP
.B HOME
Move to the first line.

.TP
.B END
Move to the last line.

.TP
.B Enter
Select and exit.

.TP
.B q
Exit without selection (return code 0).

.TP
.B x
Exit without selection (return code 10).

.TP
.BR m ", " s ", " +
Toggle mark (multiselect mode only).

.PP
Exiting with
.B x
returns exit code 10 and can be used as a “go back” signal in scripts.

.SH EXAMPLES
Select a file from a directory listing:

.PP
.nf
ls -a . | shselect -s
.fi

.PP
Select from a list of strings:

.PP
.nf
printf "%s\\n%s\\n%s\\n" "One" "Two" "Three" | shselect -sb
.fi

.PP
Possible usage in a shell script:

.PP
.nf
key=$(printf "%s" "${radiostations}" | shselect -bs)
.fi

